    "contentHtml": "<p>Breast lift and augmentation surgery (augmentation mastopexy) is a combined aesthetic surgical procedure that restores shape, an upright appearance, and fullness to breasts that have both sagged and lost volume in a single session. Factors such as aging, gravity, childbirth, breastfeeding, or significant weight changes affect breast tissue over time; sagging (ptosis) and volume loss most often occur together.</p><h3>Breast Lift and Augmentation in Istanbul</h3><p>In cases of sagging accompanied by volume loss, breast lift alone (mastopexy) cannot provide the desired fullness, and a breast augmentation implant alone (augmentation) does not correct the sagging. Meeting both of these needs in a single session makes augmentation mastopexy the right choice. At Assoc. Prof. Dr. Majid Ismayilzada's clinic in Istanbul, this combined operation, although one of the relatively complex procedures of breast aesthetics, is performed with meticulous surgical planning and personalized techniques.</p><h3>Who Is Augmentation Mastopexy Suitable For?</h3><p>This combination is an ideal solution for women who experience the following conditions together:</p><p>• Those who experience both sagging and volume loss in their breasts after breastfeeding or weight loss</p><p>• Those for whom an implant alone would not be enough to give their breasts shape, but who also need a lift</p><p>• Those who are not content with their natural volume and aim for both an upright and full appearance</p><p>• Cases where the nipple has descended below the areola and significant ptosis is experienced</p><p>In patients with only volume loss or minimal sagging, augmentation alone may be sufficient. In those with only sagging who do not want a volume change, mastopexy without an implant is performed. Which method is appropriate is decided during the examination.</p><h3>How Is Augmentation Mastopexy Performed?</h3><p>The surgery is carried out under general anesthesia and lasts approximately 2–3 hours. Since it aims to increase breast volume while reducing the skin envelope, it requires complex surgical planning.</p><p>The process consists of the following steps:</p><p>• Sagging breast tissue is reshaped and excess skin is removed.</p><p>• The nipple and areola are moved to a higher, more appropriate position aesthetically.</p><p>• A silicone implant sized to body proportions is placed in the same session under the breast tissue or the muscle.</p><p>• Incision sites are planned through the inframammary fold and around the areola so they can be hidden as much as possible.</p><p>The surgery inherently involves trade-offs: the balance between reducing the skin envelope and increasing the implant size is managed through the surgeon's experience. Therefore, individual surgical planning plays a decisive role in minimizing possible complications.</p><h3>Recovery Process After Augmentation Mastopexy</h3><p>The recovery process is somewhat more comprehensive than augmentation or mastopexy alone, as both implant and tissue healing progress together.</p><p>First 4–5 days: Mild pain, tightness, and swelling are expected. Rest and restricted movement are recommended.</p><p>1–2 weeks: A gradual return to daily activities begins.</p><p>4–6 weeks: Restrictions on heavy lifting and sports continue.</p><p>6–8 weeks: Return to full activity, including sports.</p><p>3–6 months: The time needed for the breasts to take their final form and the implants to fully settle.</p><p>The use of a special breast support garment or sports bra helps keep the implants in place and reduces swelling. Doctor follow-up appointments are important for regular monitoring of the recovery process.</p><h3>Advantages of Breast Lift and Augmentation</h3><p>• Both lifting and volume gain are achieved in a single session, avoiding the burden of two separate surgeries.</p><p>• A natural breast form in harmony with body contours is achieved.</p><p>• Torso proportions are balanced; undergarment and clothing choices become easier.</p><p>• Self-confidence and body image change positively.</p><h3>Possible Risks and Complications</h3><p>Augmentation mastopexy is one of the relatively complex operations of breast aesthetics. As with any surgical intervention, it carries certain risks:</p><p>• Asymmetry: Usually minor and can resolve during the healing process.</p><p>• Capsular contracture: Formation of hardness around the implant; rare, and treated when necessary.</p><p>• Delayed wound healing: Smoking increases this risk.</p><p>• Implant displacement: Minimized with appropriate implant selection and surgical technique.</p><p>In operations performed by an experienced surgeon and in line with recommended protocols, risks are kept to a minimum.</p><h3>Are the Results of Breast Lift and Augmentation Permanent?</h3><p>Augmentation mastopexy is a permanent procedure. As long as there is no pregnancy, breastfeeding, or significant weight change, the results maintain their aesthetic appearance for many years.</p><p>The silicone implants used are long-lasting; they can be replaced years later if necessary.</p><p>If only the sagging is to be corrected, without adding volume, you can look at the Breast Lift page.</p><h3>Why can a lift and augmentation be performed as two separate surgeries?</h3><p>In some cases, the degree of sagging and the desired implant size can increase tissue tension when performed at the same time; in such cases, for safety, the lift is performed first and the implant placement is planned 3–6 months later. The surgeon evaluates each case individually and decides.</p><h3>How long do augmentation mastopexy results last?</h3><p>As long as there is no pregnancy, breastfeeding, or significant weight change, the results can be maintained for many years. Implants can be used for a lifetime; however, slight changes can occur with the aging process. Staying at a stable weight supports permanence.</p><h3>Does this combination require a longer recovery process?</h3><p>Compared to breast augmentation alone, the recovery time is somewhat longer because both implant and tissue healing occur together. In general, compression support is applied for 6–8 weeks and heavy activity restriction for 4–6 weeks.</p><h3>In which cases is a lift alone sufficient?</h3><p>For people who are satisfied with their breast volume and only experience a sagging problem, mastopexy without an implant is sufficient. If there is no volume loss or if the patient does not want an implant, a natural and competent appearance is achieved with lifting alone.</p><h3>Does breastfeeding get affected after surgery?</h3><p>In augmentation mastopexies performed with modern techniques, care is taken not to damage the milk glands, and breastfeeding capacity is preserved in most cases. Patients who plan to breastfeed in the future should discuss this subject in detail with the surgeon during the examination.</p><h3>Which breast implant type and size is right for me?</h3><p>Implant selection is determined by body measurements, existing tissue amount, skin elasticity, and patient expectations. The choice of anatomical (teardrop) or round implants, as well as the placement plan (under the muscle or under the breast tissue), is also part of this assessment.</p>",
